## Preference → Category Mapping

This is the core logic that maps user preferences to attraction data fields:

| User Preference | Maps to Attraction Field | Value Match |
|---|---|---|
| `travelStyle: 'adventure'` | `attraction.category` | `"Adventure"`, `"Hiking"`, `"Water Sports"` |
| `travelStyle: 'culture'` | `attraction.category` | `"Heritage"`, `"Temple"`, `"Museum"` |
| `travelStyle: 'nature'` | `attraction.category` | `"Wildlife"`, `"National Park"`, `"Scenic"` |
| `travelStyle: 'relaxation'` | `attraction.category` | `"Beach"`, `"Resort"`, `"Wellness"` |
| `environment: 'mountains'` | `attraction.environment` OR `attraction.city` tags | highland cities |
| `dietary: 'seafood'` | `restaurant.cuisine_type` | `"Seafood"` |
| `activity: 'wildlife'` | `attraction.category` | `"Wildlife"`, `"Safari"` |

> [!NOTE]
> We need to check what values exist in your `Attraction_places.category` field in MongoDB. Run this in MongoDB Compass or shell to see all unique categories:
> ```js
> db.Attraction_places.distinct("category")
> ```
> Share the output and I'll map them exactly to preference IDs.

